## Why This Is Asked

Tests a robust, branchless 128-bit rotation, ensuring correct cross-boundary behavior and emphasis on manipulating split 64-bit halves.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit rotation across hi/lo halves
- Branchless implementation via masks and shifts
- Edge cases: n = 0, n = 64, n = 127

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n) {
  n %= 128;
  uint128 res;
  if (n == 0) return x; // illustrative intent; prefer mask-based approach to avoid branches
  // Branchless approach would compute both paths and select via masks
  if (n < 64) {
    res.lo = (x.lo << n) | (x.hi >> (64 - n));
    res.hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (64 - n));
  } else {
    unsigned m = n - 64;
    res.lo = (x.hi << m) | (x.lo >> (64 - m));
    res.hi = (x.lo << m) | (x.hi >> (64 - m));
  }
  return res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test edge cases like n=0, n=64, n=127? 
- How would you adapt this for a 256-bit type?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about cross-boundary 128-bit rotations without a native 128-bit type. Exercises correct handling of edge cases like r=0, r=64, and r=127, plus understanding how two 64-bit halves interact during rotation.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit numbers split into hi/lo halves
- Boundary-safe shifts across 64-bit parts
- Distinguishing r<64 vs r>=64 paths and their swap behavior

## Code Example

```c
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned r) {
  r &= 127;
  if (r == 0) return x;
  if (r < 64) {
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi << r) | (x.lo >> (64 - r));
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo << r) | (x.hi >> (64 - r));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  } else {
    unsigned t = r - 64;
    uint64_t hi = (x.lo << t) | (x.hi >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo >> (64 - t)) | (x.hi << t);
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  }
}
```

